{"kind":"report","text":"approved at exact head 5e4d19541078ac2f5a4c19780cc84b7998147f1b, request/mcp-round-trips, against artifacts 3f9d69cc (cmd/gitseq-mcp), dde19c7f (internal/statusview), 2361e099 (internal/service), efcdcfe4 (docs).\n\nCLOSURE OF THE RATIFIED CHANGES-REQUESTED REPORT da1d27f1. All four requested changes are closed at this exact head, and I verified the test by mutation rather than by reading it.\n\n1. WorkReview now carries Retired and Stale, both without omitempty, alongside Ratified, and the comment above the type was widened to cover all three. Closed.\n2. The latest exact-head review keeps last-match-wins and the row now says plainly what it is holding. My report offered two ways to close this, skipping retired verdicts or exposing the flag so the caller decides, and this head takes the second. That is the better of the two: skipping would have hidden the existence of a withdrawn verdict, which is its own silent omission, whereas the row now reads retired true and stale true on its face. Closed.\n3. docs/reference/mcp/work.md documents both new fields in the latest_review row of the field table. Closed.\n4. TestStatusAndWorkRowsCarryActionableTriageFields gained a later retired and stale approved review and now asserts report, verdict, ratified, retired, and stale on both the work page and the status lane. Closed.\n\nI did not take the test on trust. In a separate throwaway worktree at this same head I broke the seam three ways and confirmed the test fails each time, then restored the tree: replacing Retired with a constant false fails it, replacing Stale with a constant false fails it, and adding a continue that skips retired reviews when choosing the latest also fails it. The third mutation matters beyond coverage: it shows the test now pins last-match-wins as the contract, so a later change cannot flip the selection rule silently. Unmutated, the same test passes.\n\nI also measured the defect rather than assuming it was gone. At the current frontier the log holds 360 reviews, of which 11 are retired and approved and 157 are stale and approved. The number of approved reviews that would render with retired false and stale false while still being unmergeable is zero. Every approved review whose report describes a superseded world already carries the stale flag, so with these two fields no row in the live log can make an unmergeable approval look mergeable. One caveat worth writing down, not a defect in this head: that world-stale implies stale is an observed property of the fold at this frontier rather than a proven invariant, so a reader must not treat stale false as a merge guarantee. WorkReview cannot carry describes_superseded_world today because workroom.Review does not have that field; obtaining it would mean a second lookup into the statements the enrichment already scans. That is a question for whoever next touches this surface, not a condition of this review.\n\nBOTH NON-BLOCKING OBSERVATIONS. The rests_on of docs/reference/mcp/artifacts.md now names 3e42417e, which I confirmed is live and not retired at the current frontier; the page no longer starts life pointing at a withdrawn pointer. The other citation, 9627cf18 at internal/statusview, remains live and unstale. The architecture.md wrap is improved but not finished: the three lines that were ragged now fill correctly, and line 321 is still a 31-character line, \"instead of presenting a partial\", sitting mid-paragraph before a full-width line. It is cosmetic, exactly as I called it the first time, and I am not holding the head for it. One more of the same class, offered and not requested: docs/reference/mcp/status.md still describes the lane row as carrying the latest effective review \"with its explicit ratified flag\", which is now one flag of three. The page defers to work.md for the field list and work.md is correct and complete, so nothing is wrong, but the sentence has been overtaken by this head.\n\nBASE AND DIFF. Verified independently, not taken from the report. git merge-base main 5e4d1954 returns 62c5a7f4956f9d674734aafd2081800ec59953bc, which is both main and origin/main, so the branch is rebased onto current main with a clean two-commit diff. origin/request/mcp-round-trips is at 5e4d1954. The first of the two commits, d730e3e0, is a pure rebase of the previously reviewed fdb4919e: I diffed the two patches against their respective bases and they are byte-identical, so nothing changed under cover of the rebase and my first-pass reading of that content carries over exactly. The only new content is the repair commit 5e4d1954, which I read in full. All four fresh artifacts are live, record commit 5e4d1954, and carry no describes_superseded_world; the four fdb4919e candidates are properly retired.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers from docs/reference/architecture.md at this head are unchanged from my first pass: layer 6, Projections and queries, in internal/statusview, and layer 7, CLI, MCP, skills, connectors, and UI, in internal/service and cmd/gitseq-mcp. Both layer contracts change, and architecture.md is updated in this same head with the docs candidate efcdcfe4 published at this exact head, which is what the rule requires. Layers 1 to 5 remain untouched: no kernel, nexus, host-binding, or interpreter file appears in either commit, and no fold authority moves. The repair commit adds two projected fields and changes no boundary at all. projectionNotes still reports only decisions the fold already made, read from a snapshot taken after the record landed. Architecture contract preserved except where documented.\n\nSECURITY. My first-pass conclusions stand unchanged on the byte-identical first commit, and I re-state them here rather than referring to them. The bounds bind. normalizeArtifactQuery refuses an empty path list, caps at 20 paths before deduplication, refuses an empty path string, defaults limit to 20, and refuses any limit outside 1 to 50. The pagination arithmetic is correct on both edges and refuses an offset past the end rather than clamping it. Cursors convey no authority: the path set comes from the request and the cursor need only agree with its sha256 digest, so a forged cursor cannot widen a query to paths the caller did not ask for, and a negative offset, a head that does not match, and a filter that does not match are each refused. Path strings are used only as exact map keys, with no cleaning, joining, globbing, or filesystem access, so they carry no injection surface. Request size stays bounded by http.MaxBytesReader at 2 MiB with DisallowUnknownFields and guardMutation on the new POST route. laneResponseLimit is overflow-safe: the guard clamps before the multiply and the result can never exceed the 64 MiB adapter ceiling. The local fallback runs the same builder over a verified workspace snapshot and marks the answer degraded. The repair adds no untrusted input, no new route, and no new authority: two booleans copied from the projection to callers who already receive that projection, costing a few bytes per row against a ceiling measured in mebibytes. The disclosure defect I raised is closed, and closed in the direction that discloses more rather than less. No trust or authority boundary widens.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut without weakening the conditions. The repair is four lines of production code, one struct-comment revision, one documentation row, and one test row, which is the smallest shape that closes the finding; there is no new branch, no new index, and no second code path. The wider change remains a genuine reduction: dropping statementIndex from BuildWorkPage in favour of the shared enrichWorkRows means the bounded page no longer allocates a projection-sized map to label at most fifty rows, and one helper now serves BuildWorkPage, BuildActorStatus, and BuildWait where two stood before. As before I would keep the three-pass structure and keep ArtifactRow.Retired despite it always being false, for the reasons already written into the code and the page.\n\nGATES. Every gate below was run by me in a detached worktree at the exact head 5e4d19541078ac2f5a4c19780cc84b7998147f1b, not taken from the implementation report. go build ./... exit 0. go vet ./... exit 0. gofmt -l . empty. git diff --check 62c5a7f4..HEAD exit 0. go test -count=1 ./... exit 0, with all 25 packages ok, including the four the conditions name: internal/statusview at 5.7s, internal/service at 41.7s, cmd/gitseq-mcp at 77.2s, and internal/docset at 72.0s, so the documentation citation change is exercised by the docset gate and not merely eyeballed. go test -count=1 -race ./internal/statusview ./internal/service ./cmd/gitseq-mcp exit 0. No ui/ file is touched in either commit, so no UI gate applies. The mutation run described above was done in a separate worktree so it could not disturb these results, and that worktree is deleted.\n\nMERGE-STEP NOTE FOR THE REQUESTER, outside the reviewed head and not a condition of this verdict. The projection currently shows live_predecessors of 2 at cmd/gitseq-mcp, internal/statusview, and docs. At each of those paths the live predecessors are the artifact for current main plus a candidate artifact published at 921989f1, which is the unmerged staleness-presentation head serialized behind this one. Retiring another lane's in-flight candidate at step 5 would stale that lane's approval chain, so read the paths from status at merge time and retire what this merge actually changed, rather than everything the projection lists as live.\n\nCONTRACTS THE SERIALIZED LANE MUST RESPECT. This head is the agreed serialization point, and it now fixes six things that later work should build on rather than duplicate. First, latest_review is the name and object shape for review state on a row, carrying report, verdict, and the non-omitted booleans ratified, retired, and stale. Second, the selection rule for that field is last-match-wins over the projection reviews for the exact reported head, and it is now pinned by a test, so any change to it is a deliberate contract change and not an incidental one. Third, the flag vocabulary is stale, retired, and describes_superseded_world, always explicit and never inferred from an omitted field. Fourth, projected.verdict is always present on a successful state, ratify, or supersede result, including the value effective, so nothing downstream may read an absent verdict as an absent ruling. Fifth, the artifacts tool and the /v0/artifact-query endpoint are the bounded way to ask which artifacts are live at an exact path, and a staleness presentation should read that rather than downloading the whole status. Sixth, laneResponseLimit has already spent part of the adapter byte ceiling on per-row conditions, so further per-row fields consume that headroom and should be weighed against it.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3f9d69cc3b6a099f350a776c847dd8c89e3822bb","head":"5e4d19541078ac2f5a4c19780cc84b7998147f1b","stale":"true","staleness":"artifact, promise, request stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:07d4bd05c14094d1609d34a24d3df24eb491eefb, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:120642db50483bb2207b41eb3abc0a0612dde04d, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:176adca6ab73ff05662dc6e2efa8cdb1e607f98e, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:190c56e503e3d39c728c431c576f0c7864cc2e4f and 6 more","verdict":"approved"}}